MiniAi is a small chatbot written from scratch in the C programming language. It is a real neural network, not a set of templates.
The network reads text one character at a time and learns to predict the next character. When you ask a question,
the network reads it and then writes an answer, one character after another, until it decides that the answer is finished.

The network is a recurrent network of the GRU type. A GRU keeps an internal state that works like a short memory.
At every step the cell reads one character, mixes it with the old state and produces a new state.
Several layers can be stacked on top of each other, and the last layer produces a score for every possible next character.
The scores are turned into probabilities, and one character is chosen from them.

Training happens in two stages, just like in large language models. The first stage is called pretraining.
The model reads plain text from files with the extension pre and learns spelling, words and the shape of sentences.
The second stage is called fine-tuning. 
The model reads pairs of a question and an answer from files with the extension fin and learns how to reply.
Both kinds of files live in the training folder, and there can be as many of them as you like.

The weights of the network are changed by an algorithm called backpropagation through time.
After every batch the program measures how wrong the prediction was and moves every weight a little in the direction
that makes the error smaller. The size of that step is controlled by the learning rate. MiniAi uses the Adam optimizer,
which adapts the step for every weight, and it clips very large gradients so that training stays stable.

Small data is a real problem for a neural network, because it can simply memorize the examples. MiniAi fights this with dropout,
which randomly switches off some values during training, with early stopping, and with augmentation,
which creates damaged copies of the questions with a missing word or a swapped letter. Questions are also normalized,
so capital letters and punctuation do not matter.

The program is written in C because C is fast, small and available almost everywhere.
A program in C needs no virtual machine and no runtime, and it can be built with a single command.
The whole network, the training code and the console fit into a few dozen small source files,
and every file does exactly one job. Nothing is hidden inside a large library,
so a curious reader can follow every step from the first character of a question to the last character of an answer.

MiniAi works on Windows, on Linux and on Android. It was tested with the gcc compiler on Windows,
with the clang compiler on Linux and with clang inside the Termux application on Android.
The only things it needs are a C compiler and the standard library.
To build and start it you type make run, and then you type the command teach to train the model.

All settings are stored in a text file named config.yml. Every line has a name and a value, for example the number of layers,
the size of the hidden layer, the number of epochs and the learning rate. You can change a setting with the command set,
and the program saves it at once. The trained weights are saved in a file named model.bin,
so the model does not have to be trained again the next time you start the program.

The training of a network is a slow job, so MiniAi splits every batch between the threads of the processor.
Each thread works on its own part of the batch, and the results are added together at the end.
On a processor with many cores this makes training much faster. You can press the Ctrl and C keys to stop training at any time,
and the model is saved.

This code was generated by the Claude Sonnet 5.5 model for the purpose of experimenting.
It is not a replacement for a large language model. It is a small and honest example of how a neural network can learn to talk,
and it shows that even a tiny model can remember facts and answer questions when it is trained on the right data.